{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.50,
      "why": "Only 1 result/cycle families can reach 250 MSPS without heroic Fmax. Pipelined is the canonical 1/cycle CORDIC: throughput = Fmax, so 250 MSPS is easy on Artix-7. LUTs scale with N (one micro-rotation stage each) and W, so this family directly exposes the min-LUT vs accuracy trade-off. W>=14 and N>=12 bracket the region where 2^-13 error is plausible (output LSB 2^-(W-2), CORDIC angle error ~2^-N), with headroom above."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 6, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.35,
      "why": "Same 1 result/cycle throughput as pipelined, so it can also meet 250 MSPS, but registering every m stages trades Fmax for FF count. Since the objective is min LUTs (not FFs), it may not beat pipelined, but it is the main alternative architecture and worth exploring in case a coarser pipeline reduces register/LUT overhead at the same accuracy. m capped at 6 to keep the combinational path short enough that Fmax stays comfortably above 250 MHz."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 6, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.15,
      "why": "Small probe to confirm/deny feasibility. Throughput = Fmax / (ceil(N/k)+3); even at the best case k=8, N=12 this needs ceil(12/8)+3 = 5 cycles, i.e. Fmax >= 1250 MHz, which is implausible on Artix-7. Included only to let the cost model verify this rather than assume it, and to catch any edge case where a very small N still meets accuracy."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The throughput constraint (>=250 MSPS) is the binding architectural filter. iterative needs Fmax/(N+3) >= 250, i.e. >=1750 MHz even at N=4, so it is excluded outright. unrolled_k needs ceil(N/k)+3 cycles; with k<=8 and N>=12 for accuracy this still demands >1 GHz, so it gets only a small confirmation budget. pipelined and pipelined_m both deliver 1 result/cycle, so 250 MSPS only requires Fmax >= 250 MHz, which is easily achievable, and they are where the real min-LUT vs accuracy trade-off lives. Accuracy depends only on W, N, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding, so the ranges are set around the plausible 13-bit region (W 14-22, N 12-22) with the guard/rounding knobs left wide since they are cheap to explore. Budget is weighted toward pipelined (most likely min-LUT winner), then pipelined_m (main alternative), with a thin unrolled_k probe."
}

{
  "decision": "refine",
  "rationale": "Every one of the 85 feasible designs is `pipelined`; the other explored families cannot reach the 250 MSPS floor (unrolled_k max throughput 9.13 MSPS, pipelined_m max 171 MSPS, both < 250), and `iterative` is structurally worse still (1 result per N+3 cycles, so even at a generous Fmax it lands far below 250 MSPS) — so spending budget on add_family would be wasted. The binding constraint is throughput (32% violate, best seen only 273 MSPS), which only `pipelined` clears, and the selection rule is min LUTs. The current min-LUT feasible point is data_width=17/n_iter=15 at luts=1000, accuracy_bits=13.3 — only 0.3 bits of margin over the 2^-13 error limit. That thin margin is exactly where a cheaper design could hide: a smaller data_width raises Fmax (more throughput headroom) and cuts LUTs, and the front shows accuracy is jointly set by data_width and n_iter (17→20 bits of width buys only ~0.8 accuracy bits), so n_iter/guard/rounding trades may recover the lost bit. Refining tightly around the low-LUT corner is the highest-value use of the remaining 200 evals.",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {"param": "data_width", "low": 16, "high": 19, "choices": null},
          {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
          {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
          {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
          {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
        ]
      ,
      "budget_share": 100
    ,
    "why": "Push the min-LUT end of the front below 1000 LUTs while keeping throughput >= 250 and max_abs_err <= 2^-13; data_width 16-18 and n_iter 14-16 are the untested cheaper neighbours of the current winner (17/15)."
  }
  ,
  {"family": "pipelined_m", "ranges": [{"param": "data_width", "low": 16, "high": 18, "choices": null}, {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null}, {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 3, "choices": null}], "budget_share": 0.0001, "why": "Only a token probe to confirm the 171 MSPS ceiling is not beaten by m=2 at small width; expected infeasible."}
  ]
  ,
  "rationale": "Refine pipelined around the low-LUT corner; do not add iterative/unrolled_k (throughput structurally unreachable)."
} 

  }

{
  "decision": "refine",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "budget_share": 1.0,
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["3", "4"], "high": 20, "low": 16, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": ["5"], "high": 20, "low": 5, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": ["6"], "high": 5, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": ["7"], "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": ["8"], "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "k"}
        ]
     , "why": "Min-LUT feasible design sits at W=18/N=15; probe the low-area corner (W 16..19, N 14..18) to see if narrower widths offset by more iterations beat 935 LUTs while still clearing accuracy_bits>=13 and 250 MSPS."}
    ],
    "rationale": "Round-3 gain was only +0.2% and the whole feasible front is pipelined; the other explored families are throughput-starved (unrolled_k best 9.13 MSPS, pipelined_m best 171 MSPS) and the untested iterative family is structurally capped at Fmax/(N+3) results/cycle (N>=4 -> <=Fmax/7), far under the 250 MSPS floor, so it cannot enter the front and would waste the remaining 100 evals. Selection is min-LUTs, so the only payoff is pushing below the current 935-LUT / 13.04-bit point. The best feasible designs cluster at small data_width (16..19) and small n_iter (15..16), so I concentrate the last 100 evaluations there, keeping angle_guard/frac_guard open because they trade LUTs for the guard bits needed to stay under max_abs_err<=2^-13 at low width. Tightening data_width to 16..19 and n_iter to 14..18 (from the observed feasible 16..22 / 15..22) focuses the budget on the corner that actually lowers LUTs rather than re-sampling the already-mapped high-accuracy tail."
  }
  , "rationale": "Feasible front is entirely pipelined (133 feasible, best luts=935 at W=18/N=15 with accuracy_bits=13.04, throughput=264). unrolled_k and pipelined_m produced 0 feasible designs (throughput ceilings 9.13 and 171 MSPS), and iterative cannot help: at 1 micro-rotation/cycle it yields Fmax/(N+3) results/cycle, i.e. <=Fmax/7 for N>=4, structurally below the 250 MSPS constraint regardless of configuration. With a 0.2% round gain and selection = min luts, the remaining 100 evals are best spent refining the low-LUT corner of pipelined rather than adding an infeasible family."
}
